7. Using Taurus for Performance Testing
7.1 What is Taurus

Taurus is an open-source automation tool that simplifies running performance tests. It acts as a wrapper around JMeter, Gatling, and other tools, letting you define tests in a simple YAML format.

Key Benefits:

Readable, maintainable YAML configuration

Easy integration into CI/CD pipelines

Supports JMeter scripts directly

Automated reporting and metrics collection
7.2 Taurus YAML File Example
execution:
  - executor: jmeter
    scenario: sample_test
    concurrency: 50
    ramp-up: 1m
    hold-for: 5m
scenarios:
  sample_test:
    script: test_plan.jmx
reporting:
  - module: final-stats
  - module: junit-xml
  - module: console

Explanation:

executor: Load engine (JMeter)

scenario: Test scenario name

concurrency: Number of virtual users

ramp-up: Time to reach full load

hold-for: Duration to maintain load

script: JMeter test plan file

reporting: Output formats (console, JUnit XML, final stats)

7.3 Running the Test
bzt sample_test.yml
Runs the test as per YAML configuration
Generates metrics and dashboards automatically
Can run locally or on BlazeMeter cloud

7.4 Integrating Taurus with BlazeMeter
Use Taurus YAML to define your performance test
Upload the YAML file to BlazeMeter
Select load generators and execute
Monitor real-time dashboards and post-test reports
Advantage: Maintain a single Taurus file for local, CI/CD, and cloud testing.

7.5 Best Practices

Parameterize input data using CSV or environment variables

Keep scenarios modular for reusability

Combine multiple JMeter scripts in a single YAML file

Integrate with CI/CD for automated performance gates

Use BlazeMeter dashboards for detailed reporting
